# retroforge video codec links — /dev/video-enc0 for the hardware encoder. NO /dev/video-dec0.
#
# The SoCs' video block is a stateful V4L2 mem2mem device: qcom-iris on SM8250/SM8550/SM8650, venus
# on SM6115 (kernel/kernel.config). Its nodes are /dev/videoN with N in probe order.
#
# No decoder link, deliberately (user, 2026-10-29). The Steam UI's Chromium (libcef) decodes video
# through V4L2 only when /dev/video-dec0 exists, and on iris that path is worse than its CPU decode:
# the Steam UI ran four cores near 100% at 95 C on the KONKR Pocket FIT (SM8650) at its first
# sign-in, two of them its V4L2Dev threads; Nova-Deck measured it slower and ~2.5x the total CPU on
# SM8250 and no gain on SM8650 (its docs/video-decode.md), and ships no such link either. Steam's
# Remote Play client (streaming_client, CV4L2Accel) probes /dev/video0..99 as well as video-dec0..99,
# so it still finds the decoder.
# The names are each driver's video_device name (iris_probe.c, venus vdec.c/venc.c, 7.2.10); the
# nodes keep systemd's root:video 0660, and the session user is in `video`.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{name}=="qcom-iris-encoder", SYMLINK+="video-enc0"
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{name}=="qcom-venus-encoder", SYMLINK+="video-enc0"
